Below are the fields that are relevant to the requested “by‑state postal‑code vs census‑tract comparison”, with a short note on how each will be used in the final calculations.

Relevant columns: potential usage
• state_name – grouping key (state) used in all aggregations  
• count_qualified – starting point (# of buildings actually judged suitable)  
• percent_covered – % of all buildings in the region examined by Project Sunroof; used to scale count_qualified up to a state‑wide estimate  
• percent_qualified – % of examined buildings that are suitable; needed only if you want to back‑out the examined‑building denominator (covered buildings) but it cancels when you scale (see gap formula below)  
• number_of_panels_total – summed to get potential panel count (postal and tract level)  
• kw_total – summed to get total kW capacity  
• yearly_sunlight_kwh_total – summed to get yearly generation potential  
• carbon_offset_metric_tons – summed to get potential CO2 offset  
• existing_installs_count – summed to know installed buildings now  
Derived metric (gap in potential installs)  
• potential_install_gap = (count_qualified / percent_covered) – existing_installs_count  
    – percent_covered is expressed as a percentage; divide by 100 in SQL.  
    – percent_qualified drops out of the formula, so we do not need it.

Typical filtering conditions that may be useful  
• WHERE state_name IS NOT NULL  
• Possible focus on the contiguous U.S., specific states, etc.  
• When computing the gap: WHERE percent_covered > 0 to avoid division by zero.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Ten illustrative BigQuery SQL statements
(The project / dataset / table names are fixed – you can paste any query directly in the BigQuery console.  Each query is limited to 20 rows so it is quick to run and inspect.)

```sql
-- 1. List all distinct states appearing in the postal‑code table
SELECT DISTINCT state_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
ORDER BY state_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Average % covered and % qualified by state (postal‑code level)
SELECT
  state_name,
  AVG(percent_covered)  AS avg_percent_covered,
  AVG(percent_qualified) AS avg_percent_qualified
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY state_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Total qualified buildings per state (census‑tract level)
SELECT
  state_name,
  SUM(count_qualified) AS total_qualified_ct
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_censustract`
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY total_qualified_ct DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Solar potential aggregates for one sample state (Pennsylvania) from postal codes
SELECT
  state_name,
  SUM(number_of_panels_total) AS panels_total,
  SUM(kw_total)               AS kw_total,
  SUM(yearly_sunlight_kwh_total) AS kwh_total,
  SUM(carbon_offset_metric_tons) AS carbon_offset_tons
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
WHERE state_name = 'Pennsylvania'
GROUP BY state_name;
```

```sql
-- 5. Compare total qualified buildings, postal vs census, side‑by‑side
WITH postal AS (
  SELECT state_name, SUM(count_qualified) AS qualified_postal
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
  GROUP BY state_name
),
tract AS (
  SELECT state_name, SUM(count_qualified) AS qualified_tract
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_censustract`
  GROUP BY state_name
)
SELECT
  p.state_name,
  qualified_postal,
  qualified_tract
FROM postal AS p
JOIN tract  AS t
USING (state_name)
ORDER BY p.state_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Gap in potential installs (postal level) by state
SELECT
  state_name,
  SUM(count_qualified / (percent_covered/100.0)) AS est_total_suitable_buildings,
  SUM(existing_installs_count)                   AS installs_now,
  SUM(count_qualified / (percent_covered/100.0)) -
  SUM(existing_installs_count)                   AS potential_install_gap
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
WHERE percent_covered > 0
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY potential_install_gap DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Gap in potential installs (census‑tract level) by state
SELECT
  state_name,
  SUM(count_qualified / (percent_covered/100.0)) AS est_total_suitable_buildings,
  SUM(existing_installs_count)                   AS installs_now,
  SUM(count_qualified / (percent_covered/100.0)) -
  SUM(existing_installs_count)                   AS potential_install_gap
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_censustract`
WHERE percent_covered > 0
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY potential_install_gap DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Union of summary metrics (postal vs tract) for quick comparison
SELECT 'postal_code' AS level, state_name,
       SUM(count_qualified) AS qualified_buildings,
       SUM(number_of_panels_total) AS panels_total,
       SUM(kw_total) AS kw_total,
       SUM(yearly_sunlight_kwh_total) AS kwh_total
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
GROUP BY level, state_name
UNION ALL
SELECT 'census_tract', state_name,
       SUM(count_qualified),
       SUM(number_of_panels_total),
       SUM(kw_total),
       SUM(yearly_sunlight_kwh_total)
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_censustract`
GROUP BY level, state_name
ORDER BY state_name, level
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Side‑by‑side comparison of *all* required metrics, postal vs tract
WITH postal AS (
  SELECT
    state_name,
    SUM(count_qualified / (percent_covered/100.0) ) AS est_suitable_postal,
    SUM(number_of_panels_total)                     AS panels_postal,
    SUM(kw_total)                                   AS kw_postal,
    SUM(yearly_sunlight_kwh_total)                  AS kwh_postal,
    SUM(carbon_offset_metric_tons)                  AS co2_postal,
    SUM(existing_installs_count)                    AS installs_postal
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
  WHERE percent_covered > 0
  GROUP BY state_name
),
tract AS (
  SELECT
    state_name,
    SUM(count_qualified / (percent_covered/100.0) ) AS est_suitable_tract,
    SUM(number_of_panels_total)                     AS panels_tract,
    SUM(kw_total)                                   AS kw_tract,
    SUM(yearly_sunlight_kwh_total)                  AS kwh_tract,
    SUM(carbon_offset_metric_tons)                  AS co2_tract,
    SUM(existing_installs_count)                    AS installs_tract
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_censustract`
  WHERE percent_covered > 0
  GROUP BY state_name
)
SELECT
  p.state_name,
  est_suitable_postal, panels_postal, kw_postal, kwh_postal, co2_postal, installs_postal,
  est_suitable_tract,  panels_tract,  kw_tract,  kwh_tract,  co2_tract,  installs_tract
FROM postal AS p
JOIN tract  AS t USING (state_name)
ORDER BY p.state_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final comprehensive query: produce the exact set of requested by‑state metrics
SELECT
  state_name,
  -- totals at POSTAL level
  SUM(count_qualified)                       AS qualified_buildings_postal,
  AVG(percent_covered)                       AS avg_percent_covered_postal,
  AVG(percent_qualified)                     AS avg_percent_qualified_postal,
  SUM(number_of_panels_total)                AS panels_total_postal,
  SUM(kw_total)                              AS kw_total_postal,
  SUM(yearly_sunlight_kwh_total)             AS kwh_total_postal,
  SUM(carbon_offset_metric_tons)             AS co2_offset_postal,
  SUM(existing_installs_count)               AS installs_now_postal,
  -- calculated gap (postal)
  SUM(count_qualified / (percent_covered/100.0)) -
  SUM(existing_installs_count)               AS potential_install_gap_postal
FROM `bigquery-public-data.sunroof_solar.solar_potential_by_postal_code`
WHERE percent_covered > 0
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY state_name
LIMIT 20;
```

Each query focuses on a different slice or aggregation path, progressing from “quick look” to a full state‑level comparison.  You can adapt any of them (e.g., remove the `LIMIT` for complete output, or join postal and tract summaries together) to build the exact report you need.
